// Scanline fill, nonzero winding, anti-aliased.
//
// WHICH ANTI-ALIASING AND WHY. Each pixel row is sampled at 4 heights, and
// along each of those the coverage is measured in 64 columns per pixel --
// supersampling in both axes, at 4 by 64. It is not a square grid because
// the two axes cost different things: another y sample is another pass over
// every edge in the glyph, while another x column is one more cell of a
// prefix sum. So x is made fine enough that it is effectively exact -- 1/64
// of a pixel is the same resolution the coordinates themselves carry, so
// nothing finer exists to measure -- and y is kept at the 4 that a body of
// text actually needs.
//
// 4 times 64 is 256, which is also why the two numbers are these two: a
// pixel's accumulator runs 0..256 and the coverage a caller sees is
// acc * 255 / 256, so every level from 0 to 255 is reachable and the whole
// range is used. The only pair of accumulator states that share an answer is
// 0 and 1 -- a pixel touched in one of 256 places reads as untouched, which
// is the right way round for the one collision to fall.
//
// Exact x coverage per span was the alternative, and it needs the crossings
// of a scanline sorted before the winding can be accumulated. A sort with no
// break, no swap of a pair, and no record is a great deal of code for a
// result that at 1/64 pixel is not visibly different.
ras_fill() {
  int[] tab = [];
  int[] row = [];
  ras_rows(tab, row);
} return void;

// tab is the crossing accumulator, one cell per 1/64 pixel column plus one
// for crossings that fall off the right-hand end. row is one pixel row's
// coverage. Both are allocated once for the whole glyph and left zeroed by
// the code that reads them, because `id` has no way to empty a list and
// re-declaring these would rebind names that ras_rows' callees are holding.
ras_rows(int[] tab, int[] row) {
  lst_fill(tab, ras_w() * 64 + 1, 0);
  lst_fill(row, ras_w(), 0);
  ras_each(tab, row);
} return void;

ras_each(int[] tab, int[] row) {
  int r = 0;
  while(r < ras_h()) {
    r = ras_rast(r, tab, row);
  }
} return void;
